const BANK=[{"id":"w13thu_q1","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"HS follicular pathogenesis","stem":"A 27-year-old who smokes has experienced repeated painful lumps under both arms and along the groin for several years. Some lesions rupture and drain, leaving narrow openings and fibrotic scars. She has repeatedly received short antibiotic courses for presumed ordinary skin abscesses. Which underlying sequence of events best explains the combination of recurrent deep lesions and chronic tissue remodeling?","options":["Follicular occlusion followed by inflammatory nodules, sterile abscesses, sinus tracts, and scars","Primary eccrine duct obstruction with sterile sweat retention","Follicular bacterial infection progressing without chronic occlusion","Autoimmune destruction of apocrine secretory cells","Isolated inflammatory papules that heal without tissue remodeling"],"answer":"A","expl":["The lecture traces hidradenitis from pilosebaceous-unit occlusion to inflammation, abscesses, draining tunnels, and fibrosis","HS initiates in pilosebaceous follicles, not eccrine sweat ducts","HS begins with follicular occlusion and may produce sterile abscesses","The lecture describes follicular occlusion and secondary inflammation, not autoimmune gland ablation","This cannot explain chronic draining tunnels and scars"]},{"id":"w13thu_q2","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Vitiligo","stem":"A 22-year-old develops gradually expanding, sharply demarcated white patches around the mouth, hands, and eyelids. The patches have no fine scale or appreciable inflammation. The patient's medical history includes autoimmune thyroid disease. Which cellular process most directly causes the loss of pigmentation?","options":["Postinflammatory reduction in pigmentation after dermatitis","Malassezia interference with normal tanning","Congenital absence of cutaneous pigment cells","Decreased melanin transfer with preserved melanocytes","Autoimmune destruction of melanocytes"],"answer":"E","expl":["Vitiligo produces complete depigmentation from melanocyte loss rather than postinflammatory change","Tinea versicolor typically has subtle scale and truncal distribution","This is acquired autoimmune loss, not congenital hypopigmentation","The lecture attributes vitiligo to melanocyte-directed autoimmunity","Vitiligo causes true depigmentation through an autoimmune process directed at melanocytes"]},{"id":"w13thu_q3","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Mild inflammatory acne","stem":"A 19-year-old college student has experienced persistent facial and upper-back acne for nine months. Examination reveals numerous open and closed comedones interspersed with inflammatory papules and pustules. There are no deep nodules, cysts, or scars. The patient has used a nonmedicated cleanser without improvement and has never received prescription therapy. The dermatologist wants to treat the underlying follicular obstruction while simultaneously reducing inflammatory lesions, without exposing the patient to unnecessary systemic medication. Which initial combination of pharmacologic mechanisms best addresses both components of this patient's disease?","options":["Topical retinoid alone without an inflammatory-lesion treatment","Benzoyl peroxide alone without a keratinization-directed drug","Topical antibiotic alone without a retinoid","Topical retinoid plus benzoyl peroxide","Oral doxycycline alone without topical maintenance"],"answer":"D","expl":["This addresses plugging but not both components as directly as the combination","This addresses acne-associated bacteria but incompletely treats comedone formation","This does not normalize follicular plugging","The retinoid normalizes keratinization while benzoyl peroxide addresses acne-associated bacteria and inflammation; no oral agent is required for mild disease","Systemic antibiotics are more appropriate to moderate disease and do not replace topical keratinization therapy"]},{"id":"w13thu_q4","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Telogen effluvium mechanism","stem":"Four months after major abdominal surgery and a prolonged hospitalization, a 35-year-old woman notices a marked increase in hair collected while showering and brushing. Examination reveals diffuse thinning without smooth focal bald patches, scalp inflammation, or progressive frontal recession. Her hair density was previously normal. The dermatologist suspects a temporary disruption in the normal hair-growth cycle following physiological stress. Which change in the proportion of follicles occupying a particular hair-cycle phase most directly produces this patient's shedding?","options":["Increased proportion of follicles in anagen","Increased proportion of follicles in catagen alone","Progressive androgen-dependent reduction in follicle diameter","Localized T-cell-mediated follicular injury","Increased proportion of follicles in the telogen resting phase"],"answer":"E","expl":["Anagen is growth rather than the resting/shedding phase","Catagen is regression; the described shedding corresponds to telogen","This is pattern hair loss rather than abrupt post-stress shedding","This produces discrete patches of alopecia areata","Physiological stress shifts hair cycling toward the resting/shedding phase, producing diffuse loss"]},{"id":"w13thu_q5","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Rosacea topical vascular mechanism","stem":"A 41-year-old describes facial burning and intermittent flushing that becomes prominent after time outdoors and after spicy meals. Over two years, the central face has developed persistent redness with fine superficial vessels. Examination shows no comedones and few inflammatory lesions. The patient prefers a treatment that specifically diminishes visible redness between episodes. Which topical pharmacologic action would be most directly useful?","options":["Alpha-1 receptor antagonism causing vasodilation","Alpha-2 receptor antagonism causing vasodilation","Alpha-2 adrenergic receptor stimulation causing superficial vasoconstriction","Topical anti-inflammatory inhibition without vascular constriction","Selective inhibition of endothelial melanin synthesis"],"answer":"C","expl":["Widening rather than narrowing vessels would not relieve flushing","Blocking rather than activating alpha-2 receptors would not yield the described action","Brimonidine reduces erythema through alpha-2-mediated narrowing of cutaneous vessels","Anti-inflammatory therapies address papules and pustules more than immediate vascular redness","Pigment synthesis is unrelated to the prescribed rosacea vasoconstrictor"]},{"id":"w13thu_q6","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Hurley III HS tissue management","stem":"A 42-year-old woman has longstanding painful, malodorous drainage from both axillae. Examination reveals inflammatory nodules, extensive fibrotic scarring, and multiple interconnected tunnels traversing large portions of the affected skin. Topical antibiotics, antiseptic washes, and several systemic medical treatments have provided inadequate control. The dermatologist determines that the extensive network of chronically damaged tissue requires definitive local intervention rather than additional medication alone. Which procedure most directly removes the affected tissue?","options":["Additional antiseptic washes as the sole intervention","Intralesional steroid injections into isolated nodules","Topical antibiotic monotherapy","Surgical excision of extensively affected tissue","Antiandrogen monotherapy without procedural management"],"answer":"D","expl":["Washes do not remove extensive existing sinus tunnels","Local anti-inflammatory injections cannot eradicate broad interconnected tunnels","Insufficient for extensively remodeled Hurley III tissue","The lecture includes excision for extensive Hurley III disease with interconnected sinus tracts and scarring","May reduce inflammation but does not directly remove established tissue damage"]},{"id":"w13thu_q7","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Pityriasis alba","stem":"A 7-year-old with a history of atopic dermatitis develops several pale circular patches on the cheeks. The patches are mildly dry and have subtle surface scale. The parents first noticed them after summer vacation and worry that the child is permanently losing all pigment in these areas. Which diagnosis best fits this presentation?","options":["Vitiligo","Tinea versicolor","Pityriasis alba","Postinflammatory hypopigmentation","Pityriasis rosea"],"answer":"C","expl":["Complete depigmentation without subtle scale is expected","Commonly affects upper trunk in teens rather than atopic facial patches in young children","Young children with atopy, subtle facial hypopigmentation, and fine scale match pityriasis alba","There is no preceding significant inflammatory eruption at the sites to favor this","Typically a more widespread inflammatory scaly eruption rather than chronic subtle cheek patches"]},{"id":"w13thu_q8","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Hormonal acne medication","stem":"A 24-year-old woman describes inflammatory facial lesions that reliably worsen during the week preceding menstruation. Examination reveals papules and a few nodules concentrated along the chin and jawline, as well as scattered comedones. Despite adherence to topical therapy, the flares are distressing. The clinician is considering a treatment aimed at reducing the influence of circulating hormones on sebaceous glands. Which therapeutic mechanism would best accomplish that goal?","options":["Androgen receptor antagonism reducing hormone-driven sebum production","Reduced bacterial growth within the follicle","Normalized follicular keratinization without hormonal interference","Direct inhibition of 5-alpha reductase as the only described acne option","Suppression of vascular flushing through alpha-2 stimulation"],"answer":"A","expl":["Spironolactone has antiandrogen activity and is a lecture-listed option for cyclic jawline acne","Antibacterial treatments are distinct from the requested hormonal mechanism","Retinoids address obstruction but not circulating androgen stimulation","The lecture emphasizes spironolactone antiandrogen effects and OCPs for hormonal acne","This treats rosacea erythema rather than androgen-associated sebum"]},{"id":"w13thu_q9","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Primary versus secondary hyperhidrosis","stem":"A 19-year-old college student reports excessive bilateral palmar sweating since adolescence. Symptoms interfere with writing, occur several times weekly, and disappear during sleep. Her mother experienced similar symptoms. She has no constitutional complaints and takes no medications. Six months later, she develops additional generalized sweating accompanied by unexplained weight loss, heat intolerance, and palpitations. Which newly developed finding most strongly supports an acquired systemic cause of the sweating rather than progression of her original focal disorder?","options":["Worsening sweating during college examinations","More frequent symmetric palmar sweating without systemic symptoms","New generalized sweating accompanied by weight loss, heat intolerance, and palpitations","Continued absence of sweating during sleep","Further family members reporting palmar sweating"],"answer":"C","expl":["Stress can aggravate primary focal disease","Persistence of the same focal pattern still supports primary disease","New systemic symptoms suggest an underlying cause such as hyperthyroidism rather than isolated primary focal sweating","This is a supporting criterion for primary hyperhidrosis","Family history reinforces primary hyperhidrosis"]},{"id":"w13thu_q10","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Tinea capitis pathogenesis","stem":"A 12-year-old develops two patches of scalp hair loss. Unlike a sibling's previously diagnosed smooth, nonscaling bald patch, this child's lesions itch and have visible scale. Microscopic examination of scalp scrapings is planned. Which pathogenic process most likely accounts for the hair loss?","options":["Dermatophyte infection of scalp hair follicles","T-cell-mediated attack on intact scalp follicles","Androgen-dependent miniaturization of frontal follicles","Stress-related synchronous telogen shedding","Fibrotic loss of follicles after chronic traction"],"answer":"A","expl":["Scaly itchy patches with broken hairs indicate dermatophyte infection rather than smooth autoimmune alopecia","Alopecia areata is smooth and lacks fungal scale","Would cause gradual patterned thinning","Would produce diffuse shedding without scaly focal patches","Does not match itchy fungal scalp scaling and broken hairs"]},{"id":"w13thu_q11","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Phymatous rosacea histology","stem":"A 58-year-old man has longstanding episodic flushing and central facial inflammation. His nose has gradually become enlarged, irregularly contoured, and thickened, with no discrete fluctuating abscesses. Examination suggests chronic structural tissue change rather than only transient vascular dilation. Which combination of tissue processes most directly accounts for the nasal enlargement?","options":["Persistent superficial vascular dilation without glandular changes","Acute neutrophilic folliculitis with superficial pustules","Sebaceous gland hypertrophy and fibrosis from chronic inflammation","Keratin plugging producing comedones without fibrosis","Autoimmune loss of dermal pigment cells"],"answer":"C","expl":["Vasodilation alone does not explain progressive tissue hypertrophy","Superficial inflammation does not account for chronic bulky fibrosis","The lecture identifies glandular hypertrophy and fibrosis as the cause of phymatous nasal enlargement","This is an acne mechanism, not phymatous change","Would change coloration, not thicken the nose"]},{"id":"w13thu_q12","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Acne PIH prevention","stem":"A 26-year-old woman has persistent brown macules at sites where inflammatory acne lesions resolved several months earlier. Examination reveals flat areas of discoloration without active nodules or palpable scars. The patient reports repeatedly manipulating previous lesions and notices that the discoloration becomes more apparent after sun exposure. The dermatologist explains that the marks often improve gradually but that environmental exposure can prolong their visibility. Which daily preventive intervention most directly addresses this aggravating factor?","options":["Frequent chemical exfoliation with deliberate picking","Immediate surgical removal of flat marks","Stopping all acne treatment once the marks appear","Prolonged ultraviolet exposure to even skin tone","Daily sunscreen and prevention of additional acne lesions"],"answer":"E","expl":["Repeated irritation and picking exacerbate PIH","Excision is not recommended for flat postacne macules","Ongoing lesions can produce additional postinflammatory pigmentation","UV can increase contrast and worsen visible pigmentation","The lecture emphasizes sun protection and preventing new lesions while PIH fades over months"]},{"id":"w13thu_q13","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Anticholinergics in sweating","stem":"A 25-year-old has persistent, socially disabling palmar sweating despite an adequate trial of topical aluminum chloride. After discussing alternatives, the clinician prescribes a medication intended to reduce eccrine gland secretion by blocking the postsynaptic response to autonomic cholinergic input. Which receptor-level mechanism is responsible for the expected decrease in sweating?","options":["Inhibition of acetylcholine release from nerve terminals","Competitive blockade of muscarinic receptors on eccrine sweat glands","Permanent destruction of eccrine sweat glands","Physical blockade of sweat-gland openings","Cholinergic receptor agonism at eccrine glands"],"answer":"B","expl":["This is the distinct botulinum toxin mechanism","Glycopyrrolate and oxybutynin inhibit sweat-gland cholinergic stimulation","Oral antimuscarinics do not ablate glands","This is topical aluminum chloride","Activation would stimulate rather than reduce sweating"]},{"id":"w13thu_q14","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Tinea versicolor pigment mechanism","stem":"An 18-year-old develops multiple finely scaly patches over the chest and upper back. During the summer, the lesions become increasingly conspicuous because the surrounding skin darkens while the affected patches remain pale. Microscopy reveals superficial fungal elements, without evidence of a deeper invasive infection. A dermatologist explains that the pigmentary changes arise partly from interference with normal tanning rather than destruction of melanocytes. Which alteration in cutaneous pigmentation most directly accounts for the clinical appearance?","options":["Immune destruction of all melanocytes in each lesion","Melanin synthesis increases within yeast-affected epidermis","Increased ultraviolet tanning in lesions compared with surrounding skin","Follicular keratinization selectively blocks visible pigment","Decreased melanin synthesis and reduced tanning in the affected skin"],"answer":"E","expl":["Vitiligo, not Malassezia, produces complete melanocyte loss","Increased melanin would make patches darker","Affected sites show decreased tanning","The lecture specifies impaired melanin synthesis/tanning, not keratin obstruction","Malassezia interferes with pigmentation and tanning, explaining summer contrast"]},{"id":"w13thu_q15","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Moderate acne antibiotic escalation","stem":"A 21-year-old has persistent acne despite several months of regular topical treatment. Examination demonstrates comedones, widespread inflammatory papules and pustules, and multiple tender nodules involving the cheeks and upper back. There is no extensive cystic disease or disfiguring scarring. The clinician elects to retain the topical treatment directed at follicular plugging and add systemic therapy. Which additional treatment is most consistent with the lecture's approach to disease of this severity?","options":["Oral isotretinoin immediately for all moderate acne","Oral doxycycline added to topical retinoid and benzoyl peroxide","Addition of topical retinoid alone after current failure","Topical antibiotic monotherapy replacing both current topicals","Oral antifungal therapy"],"answer":"B","expl":["Reserved primarily for severe nodulocystic/scarring cases","The lecture lists oral doxycycline for moderate inflammatory acne while continuing topical treatments","He is already using a retinoid and needs escalation for inflammatory severity","Insufficient escalation and omits ongoing keratinization therapy","Acne-associated inflammation is not a dermatophyte infection"]},{"id":"w13thu_q16","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"JAK inhibitor for widespread alopecia","stem":"A 28-year-old with autoimmune thyroid disease develops several well-circumscribed bald patches on the scalp over two months. The skin is smooth without itch or scale. Over the next month, additional patches appear despite local therapy. Which intracellular signaling pathway is targeted by the systemic immunomodulatory treatment described for progressive disease?","options":["TNF-alpha neutralization","IL-17 neutralization","5-alpha reductase inhibition","Topical keratinization normalization","JAK/STAT signaling"],"answer":"E","expl":["This is adalimumab action, not the lecture-listed tofacitinib target","This describes secukinumab action rather than tofacitinib","This treats androgenetic hair loss rather than T-cell-mediated alopecia","This is a retinoid action in acne","The lecture identifies tofacitinib as a JAK/STAT inhibitor used in extensive alopecia areata"]},{"id":"w13thu_q17","lecture":10,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Hurley stage distinction","stem":"In the Hurley classification of hidradenitis suppurativa, what anatomical feature distinguishes Stage II disease from Stage I disease?","options":["Inflammatory nodules without any tunneling","Diffuse increased nodule count alone","Development of sinus tracts and scarring","Interconnected tunnels across an entire region","A new lesion in the opposite axilla without scars"],"answer":"C","expl":["This is Hurley I","Hurley II requires tracts/scarring, not simply more lesions","Hurley I lacks tunnels/scars; Stage II has limited tracts/scars","This better identifies Hurley III","Distribution alone does not establish Stage II"]},{"id":"w13thu_q18","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Retinoid effect","stem":"A 16-year-old has numerous noninflammatory follicular lesions on the forehead and chin, with only occasional superficial papules. A topical vitamin A derivative is prescribed, and the clinician explains that treatment must be continued for several weeks before its full effect can be assessed. During that period, the medication acts on an early step in lesion development rather than directly targeting established bacterial inflammation. Which change in follicular physiology is most responsible for preventing additional lesions?","options":["Normalization of follicular keratinization and reduced plugging","Decreased bacterial proliferation with no effect on follicular epithelium","Suppression of androgen stimulation of sebocytes","Reduced inflammatory cytokines through TNF-alpha blockade","Direct blockade of sebocyte acetylcholine receptors"],"answer":"A","expl":["Topical retinoids reduce comedones by normalizing desquamation within follicular ducts","This more directly describes benzoyl peroxide","This is an antiandrogen mechanism","Not the action of topical retinoids in comedonal acne","Anticholinergics are not the retinoid mechanism"]},{"id":"w13thu_q19","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Vitiligo autoimmune association","stem":"A 29-year-old has well-defined depigmented macules around both eyes and over several fingertips. There is no erythema or scale. During the review of systems, the physician asks about symptoms of thyroid dysfunction and other autoimmune conditions rather than pursuing fungal testing. Which associated autoimmune disorder should be considered in light of her cutaneous findings and symptoms?","options":["Graves disease with overt hyperthyroidism","Primary hypothyroidism due solely to iodine excess","Subclinical hyperthyroidism","Hashimoto thyroiditis causing hypothyroidism","Nonautoimmune thyroid nodule producing excess hormone"],"answer":"D","expl":["The symptoms favor reduced rather than excess thyroid function","The clinical autoimmune association points toward Hashimoto rather than this mechanism","Does not readily explain cold intolerance and constipation","Depigmentation plus fatigue, cold intolerance, and constipation is compatible with associated autoimmune hypothyroidism","Thyroid hyperfunction would more often cause heat intolerance and palpitations"]},{"id":"w13thu_q20","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Botulinum mechanism","stem":"A 33-year-old receives an injectable treatment for severe focal axillary sweating after topical regimens fail. Sweating diminishes substantially, but the dermatologist warns that the injections must generally be repeated after several months. The therapy acts upstream of glandular receptors rather than mechanically blocking sweat ducts. Which neurotransmission step is directly inhibited by this treatment?","options":["Postsynaptic muscarinic receptor blockade","Physical blockage of sweat ducts","Increased presynaptic acetylcholine release","Permanent denervation of the eccrine glands","Inhibition of presynaptic acetylcholine release"],"answer":"E","expl":["This is glycopyrrolate or oxybutynin mechanism","This is aluminum chloride mechanism","Would stimulate sweating rather than suppress it","Botulinum treatment is temporary and repeatable, not permanent nerve removal","Botulinum toxin reduces eccrine activation by limiting release of acetylcholine from cholinergic nerve terminals"]},{"id":"w13thu_q21","lecture":10,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Acne sequela","stem":"A teenager with persistent inflammatory acne is concerned about permanent changes in skin appearance. Which long-term structural complication of acne is particularly important to prevent through early control of severe inflammatory lesions?","options":["Transient postinflammatory redness","Postinflammatory hyperpigmentation","Temporary epidermal dryness","Permanent scarring","Reversible inflammatory pustules"],"answer":"D","expl":["Redness is not the same as a permanent structural sequela","Can persist but is primarily discoloration rather than a permanent structural defect","A symptom rather than lasting tissue damage","Severe inflammatory acne can leave irreversible scar formation, making early control important","An active lesion, not a permanent outcome"]},{"id":"w13thu_q22","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Female androgenetic alopecia mechanism","stem":"A 31-year-old woman reports a widening central hair part over several years, while the frontal hairline remains relatively preserved. Her mother has similar thinning. Examination reveals gradual reduction in hair density over the crown without inflamed scaly patches or discrete smooth areas of hair loss. Which progressive structural change within affected follicles accounts for this hair-loss pattern?","options":["Abrupt T-cell-mediated destruction of follicles","Shift of otherwise normal follicles into telogen","Progressive miniaturization of androgen-sensitive follicles","Infection of hair shafts with dermatophytes","Scarring obliteration of follicular openings"],"answer":"C","expl":["Creates smooth alopecia areata patches","Produces temporary diffuse shedding","Androgenetic alopecia leads to thinner follicles and characteristic widening of the part line","Causes scaling and itching","Would result in irreversible scarring alopecia, unlike the gradual pattern described"]},{"id":"w13thu_q23","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Biologic HS target pairing","stem":"A 31-year-old woman has recurrent painful axillary and inguinal nodules with several draining sinus tracts and fibrotic scars despite topical and oral antibiotics. The dermatologist recommends a systemic biologic agent that reduces inflammation through neutralization of a proinflammatory cytokine. Before treatment, the physician explains that the selected medication targets a different inflammatory pathway from secukinumab. Which medication–molecular target pairing is appropriate for the proposed therapy?","options":["Secukinumab — TNF-alpha","Adalimumab — IL-17A","Infliximab — IL-17A","Adalimumab — tumor necrosis factor-alpha","Secukinumab — IL-4 receptor"],"answer":"D","expl":["Secukinumab targets IL-17A","Adalimumab targets TNF-alpha","Infliximab targets TNF-alpha rather than IL-17","Adalimumab inhibits TNF-alpha; secukinumab targets IL-17A rather than TNF-alpha","Secukinumab targets IL-17A, not IL-4 signaling"]},{"id":"w13thu_q24","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Early acne morphology","stem":"In an experimental pilosebaceous unit, keratinocytes within the follicle become unusually cohesive while sebaceous secretion increases. The gland remains open initially, but material gradually accumulates at the follicular opening. What lesion should form first, before extensive neutrophilic inflammation develops?","options":["Inflammatory pustule","Deep inflammatory nodule","Open or closed comedone","Sinus tract","Fibrotic depressed scar"],"answer":"C","expl":["Develops with more established follicular inflammation","Represents more severe later inflammation","Retention of keratin and sebum produces follicular comedones before overt inflammatory lesions","Develops in chronic HS, not earliest comedonal acne","A sequela of inflammation rather than earliest lesion"]},{"id":"w13thu_q25","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Topical tinea versicolor therapy","stem":"A 17-year-old student develops numerous finely scaly hypopigmented patches across the upper chest and back. The lesions become more conspicuous during summer, and microscopic examination reveals superficial yeast forms. The eruption is limited to the skin, without systemic illness or extensive treatment-resistant disease. The dermatologist chooses a first-line topical treatment rather than systemic therapy. Which medication class is most appropriate?","options":["Topical azole antifungal","Topical retinoid","Topical corticosteroid monotherapy","Topical JAK inhibitor","Topical antibacterial clindamycin"],"answer":"A","expl":["The lecture lists topical azoles for Malassezia-related tinea versicolor","Treats comedonal acne rather than Malassezia","May reduce redness but does not eradicate the yeast","Treats vitiligo and does not target superficial yeast","Targets acne-associated bacteria, not Malassezia yeast"]},{"id":"w13thu_q26","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Ocular rosacea supportive treatment","stem":"A 49-year-old woman with a history of recurrent facial flushing reports persistent gritty eyes, increased blinking, and discomfort worsening throughout the day. Examination demonstrates eyelid-margin inflammation and mild conjunctival injection without purulent discharge, corneal injury, or acute visual loss. Her symptoms are mild and primarily related to ocular surface dryness. Which initial supportive treatment most directly addresses the dominant symptoms?","options":["Low-dose doxycycline as the only immediate intervention","Artificial tears","Pulsed dye laser","Topical metronidazole applied to eyelid mucosa","Surgical treatment of eyelid glands"],"answer":"B","expl":["Can help ocular inflammation but tears most directly target predominant dryness","For mild predominantly dry/gritty ocular rosacea, artificial tears are a lecture-listed supportive treatment","Treats facial telangiectasias, not ocular dryness","Cutaneous rosacea therapy is not the initial supportive eye-drop treatment","Not indicated for uncomplicated mild ocular surface dryness"]},{"id":"w13thu_q27","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Alopecia totalis","stem":"A teenager has round, smooth, nonscarring patches of hair loss and subsequently loses all remaining scalp hair while eyelashes and hair elsewhere on the body are retained. Which named extent-of-disease category now applies?","options":["Alopecia universalis","Multifocal localized alopecia areata","Telogen effluvium","Alopecia totalis","Androgenetic alopecia"],"answer":"D","expl":["Would affect hair outside the scalp as well","Scalp involvement is now complete, not merely discrete patches","Diffuse shedding does not define complete scalp hair loss","Loss of all scalp hair with body hair preserved is totalis; universalis involves all body hair","Gradual patterned thinning, not complete scalp loss"]},{"id":"w13thu_q28","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Topical retinoid timing","stem":"An adolescent began treatment with a topical vitamin A derivative for predominantly noninflammatory facial lesions. After three weeks of consistent application, the number of existing bumps appears largely unchanged, although no major adverse effects have occurred. The patient's family wants to discontinue the prescription because they believe the drug cannot alter the disease. How should the expected time course and mechanism of this therapy guide the response?","options":["Continue treatment and reassess after at least six weeks of consistent use","Discontinue after three weeks as definitive failure","Begin oral isotretinoin immediately for minor residual comedones","Switch to oral antibiotics for all remaining comedones","Use only topical antibiotics instead of the retinoid"],"answer":"A","expl":["The lecture emphasizes that topical retinoids require at least six weeks to show efficacy","The lecture says topical retinoids take at least six weeks","No severe nodulocystic disease warrants this escalation","Oral antibiotics do not address the basic keratin-plug problem","Antibiotics alone do not normalize follicular plugging"]},{"id":"w13thu_q29","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Hurley II systemic treatment","stem":"A 32-year-old woman has recurrent painful nodules and abscesses in the inframammary folds and groin. Examination reveals several separate draining sinus tracts with localized scarring, but no extensive interconnected tunnels. The disease remains active despite antiseptic washes and multiple courses of oral antibiotics. The dermatologist decides to escalate to a biologic medication specifically identified in the lecture as an option for this disease stage. Which medication is most appropriate?","options":["Oral isotretinoin","Adalimumab","Topical clindamycin alone","Oral spironolactone as the sole proposed biologic","Intralesional steroids as definitive biologic escalation"],"answer":"B","expl":["Used for severe acne, not an identified Hurley II biologic","The lecture specifically lists TNF-alpha inhibitor adalimumab as an option for Hurley II refractory HS","Insufficient after failed local and systemic therapy","It is an antiandrogen, not a biologic","Local steroid injections are not biologic therapy"]},{"id":"w13thu_q30","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"5 alpha reductase inhibitor","stem":"A 39-year-old man has progressively lost hair from the frontal scalp and vertex over a decade. There is no sudden shedding, recent illness, or scalp inflammation. Several male relatives have had similar hair loss. The clinician offers treatment directed at androgen-dependent effects on follicles rather than immunosuppression or antifungal therapy. Which enzyme is inhibited by the prescribed antiandrogen therapy?","options":["Androgen receptor","JAK kinase","Aromatase","Tyrosinase","5-alpha reductase"],"answer":"E","expl":["Spironolactone has antiandrogen effects; finasteride inhibits an enzyme instead","Tofacitinib inhibits JAK signaling in autoimmune alopecia","Not the enzyme inhibited by finasteride/dutasteride","Involved in melanin production, not the targeted androgen conversion","Finasteride and dutasteride inhibit 5-alpha reductase, reducing androgen effects involved in follicular miniaturization"]},{"id":"w13thu_q31","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Rosacea immediate change","stem":"A patient with persistent facial erythema receives a topical medication that stimulates alpha-2 adrenergic receptors in superficial blood vessels. The treatment produces a transient reduction in vessel diameter without directly altering inflammatory cell activity or follicular keratinization. Which clinical feature would be expected to improve most immediately following administration?","options":["Immediate disappearance of inflammatory papules","Reduced background facial erythema","Resolution of fixed nasal fibrosis","Decreased numbers of comedones","Permanent elimination of telangiectasias"],"answer":"B","expl":["Alpha-2 agonism primarily narrows vessels, not inflammatory lesions","Alpha-2-mediated vasoconstriction can promptly diminish visible background redness","Vascular tone cannot reverse structural phymatous hypertrophy","Keratin-plug treatment rather than vascular constriction is needed","Temporary vasoconstriction does not necessarily eradicate structural vessels"]},{"id":"w13thu_q32","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Secondary hyperhidrosis cause","stem":"A 54-year-old has begun experiencing generalized sweating, including episodes that wake him from sleep. The symptoms are new, unlike the focal palmar sweating he occasionally experienced as a teenager. He recently started a medication for depression and also reports intermittent palpitations and weight loss. Which underlying disorder should be prioritized as a potentially reversible secondary cause of his sweating?","options":["Medication-independent primary focal hyperhidrosis","Hypoglycemia alone","Febrile illness without fever","Hyperthyroidism","Lymphoma without constitutional or mass findings"],"answer":"D","expl":["The new onset with nocturnal symptoms and systemic findings makes this less likely","Some hypoglycemic drugs can cause sweating, but heat intolerance and weight loss favor hyperthyroidism","Fever is absent and the endocrine syndrome is more specific","Generalized sweating accompanied by weight loss, palpitations, and heat intolerance warrants assessment for hyperthyroidism","Lymphoma is a possible secondary cause but the thyroid symptom cluster is more characteristic"]},{"id":"w13thu_q33","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Isotretinoin pregnancy monitoring","stem":"A 20-year-old woman with extensive nodulocystic acne and early scarring has not improved sufficiently with topical retinoids and systemic antibiotics. Her dermatologist plans to begin an oral vitamin A derivative and explains that treatment requires participation in a formal pregnancy-prevention monitoring program. Baseline laboratory testing shows normal hepatic enzymes and triglycerides. Which additional safety assessment must be incorporated repeatedly during treatment because of the medication's potential effects on fetal development?","options":["Monthly pregnancy testing","Annual pregnancy testing","Pregnancy testing only before the first dose","No pregnancy testing if liver enzymes remain normal","Lipid testing as the sole reproductive risk assessment"],"answer":"A","expl":["The lecture requires monthly pregnancy testing during isotretinoin therapy for patients who can become pregnant because of severe teratogenicity","The lecture specifies monthly, not annual testing","Baseline-only surveillance would miss pregnancy during therapy","Hepatic laboratory monitoring does not replace teratogenicity precautions","Lipids detect metabolic adverse effects rather than pregnancy"]},{"id":"w13thu_q34","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Pigment differential test","stem":"An 18-year-old develops multiple pale patches across the upper chest and back. The patches are slightly scaly and became more noticeable after a summer vacation. She has no history of eczema, and the facial skin is unaffected. A younger sibling has pale facial patches associated with atopic dermatitis. The clinician suspects that the two patients have different causes of their pigmentation changes. Which diagnostic investigation would most directly distinguish the older patient's suspected condition from the sibling's atopy-associated disorder?","options":["Wood lamp inspection without collecting scale","Microscopic examination of skin scrapings for superficial fungal elements","Serum thyroid autoantibody testing","Punch biopsy to evaluate melanocyte destruction","Bacterial swab culture of intact hypopigmented patches"],"answer":"B","expl":["The lecture explicitly supports scrape/microscopy for suspected fungal involvement","Scraping can support fungal tinea versicolor and distinguish it from atopy-related pityriasis alba","Autoimmune evaluation is more relevant to vitiligo, not scaly truncal yeast lesions","More invasive and not the lecture-described first-line test for yeast","The likely cause is yeast, not bacterial infection"]},{"id":"w13thu_q35","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Localized areata treatment","stem":"A 14-year-old is brought to clinic because of a single circular patch of hair loss on the scalp that appeared over several weeks. The underlying skin is smooth and asymptomatic, without scale, redness, or broken inflamed follicles. Several family members have autoimmune disease. Which local treatment is most appropriate for this limited form of nonscarring hair loss?","options":["Oral terbinafine for presumed fungal infection","Oral finasteride for follicular miniaturization","Intralesional corticosteroid injection into affected scalp patches","Observation followed by treatment only if all scalp hair is lost","Surgical excision of alopecic scalp"],"answer":"C","expl":["No scale/itch supports autoimmune alopecia over tinea capitis","Patterned androgen-related hair loss is not present","The lecture lists intralesional or topical corticosteroids for localized alopecia areata","Localized areata can be treated and does not require waiting for progression","Not appropriate for nonscarring autoimmune patches"]},{"id":"w13thu_q36","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Rosacea vessels procedure","stem":"A 38-year-old reports diffuse facial flushing after hot weather and spicy foods. Physical examination demonstrates persistent central erythema and small telangiectasias, but no comedones or deep follicular nodules. The clinician proposes sunscreen, gentle skin care, and avoidance of provoking exposures. If the patient later requests a procedure aimed at the persistent abnormal superficial vessels, which procedure is most appropriate from the lecture's treatment options?","options":["Pulsed dye laser treatment","Topical metronidazole as the only residual-vessel therapy","Topical ivermectin to eliminate Demodex","Oral low-dose doxycycline for visible vessels","Surgical excision of nasal tissue"],"answer":"A","expl":["The lecture lists pulsed dye laser for persistent telangiectasias in erythematotelangiectatic rosacea","Targets papulopustular inflammation, which already improved","Targets some inflammatory papulopustular disease, not fixed telangiectasias","Anti-inflammatory treatment is not the most direct procedural vascular intervention","Used for phymatous disease, which is not described"]},{"id":"w13thu_q37","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"HS lifestyle measures","stem":"A 23-year-old man presents with recurrent inflammatory nodules in both axillae. No sinus tracts or scars are present. He has obesity and smokes cigarettes daily. Alongside medication for the active lesions, the dermatologist recommends behavioral interventions that may reduce disease burden and future complications. Which combination of modifiable risk factors should be addressed regardless of the patient's Hurley stage?","options":["Stopping topical therapy once lesions become asymptomatic","Avoiding UV light and spicy foods","Smoking cessation and weight loss","Daily antifungal shampoo and weight loss","Smoke cessation without addressing excess weight"],"answer":"C","expl":["Does not address obesity and smoking risk factors","Rosacea triggers rather than the main HS risk-factor targets","The lecture emphasizes weight loss and tobacco cessation across all Hurley stages","Antifungal shampoo does not treat HS disease mechanisms","The lecture emphasizes both measures across stages"]},{"id":"w13thu_q38","lecture":10,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Acne diagnostic lesion","stem":"For a patient with facial papules and pustules, which additional primary lesion is the most useful diagnostic finding for identifying acne vulgaris rather than another inflammatory acneiform disorder?","options":["Comedone","Telangiectasia","Papule","Pustule","Flushing erythema"],"answer":"A","expl":["The presence of comedones is the defining diagnostic anchor of acne vulgaris in the lecture","Suggests vascular rosacea","Occurs in both acne and papulopustular rosacea","Occurs in both acne and rosacea","Suggests rosacea and does not establish acne"]},{"id":"w13thu_q39","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Telogen effluvium prognosis","stem":"A 23-year-old has sudden diffuse shedding several months after a major stressful illness, whereas a 44-year-old has a slowly widening part line over years and a strong family history of thinning hair. Neither has scalp erythema, pruritus, or scale. Which clinical course is most likely for the younger patient's stress-associated diffuse shedding?","options":["Permanent progressive miniaturization over several years","Relapsing scaly focal areas that require antifungal therapy","Spread from patches to complete body hair loss","Persistent irreversible follicle destruction following a single stressor","Spontaneous improvement and recovery over approximately 6–12 months"],"answer":"E","expl":["Expected with androgenetic alopecia","Characteristic of untreated tinea capitis","Could describe extensive alopecia areata, not telogen effluvium","Telogen effluvium is a reversible hair-cycle shift","The lecture describes telogen effluvium as self-resolving within six to twelve months"]},{"id":"w13thu_q40","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Papulopustular rosacea therapy","stem":"A 46-year-old woman has recurrent central facial papules and pustules over a background of persistent erythema. Symptoms worsen following alcohol consumption and sun exposure. There are no comedones or prominent telangiectasias. The physician considers several treatments used for different rosacea manifestations, including agents directed at vascular dilation, inflammatory lesions, and chronic tissue hypertrophy. Which topical treatment is most appropriate for the dominant active lesions in this patient?","options":["Topical brimonidine","Topical adapalene","Pulsed dye laser","Topical metronidazole","Surgical excision of nasal hypertrophy"],"answer":"D","expl":["Targets background erythema rather than the dominant inflammatory papules","Targets acne comedonal keratinization, which is not the dominant lesion or even present","Targets telangiectasias, which are not prominent","The lecture lists topical metronidazole for papulopustular rosacea for its anti-inflammatory action","Not relevant without phymatous changes"]},{"id":"w13thu_q41","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Hurley progression","stem":"A patient initially has recurrent tender nodules in one axilla without sinus tracts or scarring. Over the following year, examination reveals a localized draining tunnel and fibrotic changes, although the lesions do not form a broad interconnected network. How should the patient's Hurley stage be reclassified following this progression?","options":["Hurley I to Hurley III","Hurley II to Hurley III","Hurley III to Hurley II","No change in stage despite tunnel formation","Hurley I to Hurley II"],"answer":"E","expl":["Stage III requires interconnected extensive tracts","The initial case was Stage I rather than II","The course is progression from initial Stage I","A new tunnel and scar change classification to II","Appearance of a localized tract and scar moves HS from Stage I into Stage II"]},{"id":"w13thu_q42","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Vitiligo immunomodulatory therapy","stem":"A 37-year-old with stable, sharply demarcated depigmented patches on the hands asks about treatment options after topical skin care alone has not improved the appearance. Examination shows no scale or signs of an active superficial infection. The dermatologist discusses immune-modulating topical treatment and a controlled light-based therapy. Which topical drug that inhibits intracellular inflammatory signaling is a treatment option for this condition?","options":["Topical corticosteroid","Topical ruxolitinib","Topical calcineurin inhibitor","Topical antifungal azole","Narrowband ultraviolet B therapy"],"answer":"B","expl":["A valid alternate vitiligo treatment but not a JAK-inhibiting medication","Ruxolitinib inhibits JAK signaling and is a lecture-listed treatment for vitiligo","Immunomodulatory but not the named JAK-inhibiting treatment on the slides","Targets yeast rather than autoimmune depigmentation","Effective vitiligo therapy but not a topical JAK-inhibiting medication"]},{"id":"w13thu_q43","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Nodulocystic acne escalation","stem":"A 17-year-old has several years of acne with numerous deep, painful facial nodules, inflammatory cysts, and early depressed scars. Courses of topical retinoids, benzoyl peroxide, and oral antibiotics have provided only temporary control. The patient is increasingly avoiding school activities because of the appearance and discomfort. Which systemic treatment is most appropriate to limit further severe disease and permanent scarring?","options":["Continuation of topical retinoid alone","Another trial of oral doxycycline alone","Topical benzoyl peroxide without systemic therapy","Hormonal antiandrogen monotherapy","Oral isotretinoin"],"answer":"E","expl":["Already inadequate for severe nodulocystic disease","He has persistent cysts and scars despite oral antibiotics","Insufficient to control deep scarring acne","Not the principal intervention for this severe presentation","The lecture recommends isotretinoin for severe nodulocystic and scarring acne that persists despite topical and antibiotic treatment"]},{"id":"w13thu_q44","lecture":10,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Resting hair cycle phase","stem":"Which phase of the normal hair-growth cycle is considered the resting phase?","options":["Anagen","Catagen","Exogen","Telogen","Early anagen"],"answer":"D","expl":["Active growth phase","Regression phase","Hair shedding terminology, not the lecture-listed resting phase","The lecture lists anagen growth, catagen regression, and telogen resting phases","Part of active growth rather than the resting phase"]},{"id":"w13thu_q45","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Aluminum chloride mechanism","stem":"A topical antiperspirant preparation containing 20% aluminum chloride reduces bothersome axillary sweating without altering the patient's circulating thyroid hormone concentration or autonomic nerve signaling. Which local physical effect accounts for its benefit?","options":["Physical occlusion of eccrine sweat gland ducts","Muscarinic receptor antagonism","Presynaptic acetylcholine blockade","Destruction of sweat glands by laser","Inhibition of cholinergic nerve conduction"],"answer":"A","expl":["Topical aluminum chloride reduces sweat output by physically occluding eccrine glands","The mechanism of glycopyrrolate/oxybutynin rather than aluminum chloride","The action of botulinum toxin","Not the lecture-described mechanism of aluminum chloride","Aluminum chloride acts locally by occlusion, not neural inhibition"]},{"id":"w13thu_q46","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Rosacea Demodex treatment","stem":"A 36-year-old woman has persistent central facial papules and pustules despite consistent trigger avoidance and gentle skin care. Examination reveals background erythema without comedones or phymatous changes. A prior topical anti-inflammatory treatment produced only partial improvement. The dermatologist suspects that an exaggerated immune response to organisms inhabiting facial follicles contributes to the remaining lesions and selects a treatment with antiparasitic activity. Which topical medication best addresses this proposed mechanism?","options":["Topical metronidazole","Topical brimonidine","Low-dose doxycycline","Topical ivermectin","Topical retinoid"],"answer":"D","expl":["Anti-inflammatory and a valid alternative rosacea agent but not the specified antiparasitic therapy","Primarily reduces vascular erythema","Anti-inflammatory but not a topical antiparasitic","The lecture lists ivermectin as a topical agent that targets Demodex mites implicated in papulopustular rosacea","Treats acne comedones rather than the proposed mite target"]},{"id":"w13thu_q47","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Tinea capitis route","stem":"A 10-year-old has gradually enlarging patches of itchy scalp hair loss. Examination shows scaling over the affected areas, and microscopy of collected scale reveals fungal elements. The family asks whether a topical corticosteroid will restore hair growth. Which treatment route and drug category are appropriate for the underlying cause of the alopecia?","options":["Topical antifungal shampoo alone","Systemic oral antifungal therapy such as griseofulvin or terbinafine","Topical hydrocortisone","Intralesional corticosteroid","Oral finasteride"],"answer":"B","expl":["A topical-only regimen is inadequate for follicle infection; oral therapy is necessary","Hair follicle dermatophyte infection requires oral antifungal therapy for a lecture-listed 4–8-week course","Reduces local inflammation but does not eradicate fungi","Would be directed at alopecia areata rather than fungus","Treats androgenetic alopecia, not infection"]},{"id":"w13thu_q48","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"BPO added to retinoid","stem":"A 20-year-old man with facial acne has been using a topical retinoid for several months. Follow-up examination reveals a substantial reduction in comedones, but inflammatory papules and pustules persist. The clinician decides to add a topical agent that acts on a different component of acne pathogenesis without introducing systemic antibiotics. Which additional therapeutic action would best complement his existing medication?","options":["Further reduction in keratinocyte cohesion only","Selective suppression of sebocyte androgen receptors","Reduction of acne-associated bacterial proliferation","Inhibition of anti-inflammatory cytokines via JAK blockade","Direct inhibition of 5-alpha reductase"],"answer":"C","expl":["Already the principal effect of the retinoid","A hormonal rather than benzoyl peroxide mechanism","Benzoyl peroxide targets acne-related bacteria and complements retinoid reduction of follicular obstruction","Not benzoyl peroxide action","A hair-loss therapy and unrelated to the antimicrobial complement"]},{"id":"w13thu_q49","lecture":10,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Primary hyperhidrosis pattern","stem":"Which two clinical characteristics concerning distribution and sleep are commonly used to support a diagnosis of primary focal hyperhidrosis?","options":["Focal sweating that persists throughout sleep","Bilateral focal sweating that stops during sleep","Generalized sweating with nocturnal drenching","Unilateral sweating starting in late adulthood","New sweating following systemic medication initiation"],"answer":"B","expl":["Primary focal sweating typically stops during sleep","Symmetrical localized sweating with absence during sleep is characteristic of primary hyperhidrosis","Prompts evaluation for secondary causes","Not the characteristic symmetrical young-onset pattern","Supports medication-related secondary hyperhidrosis"]},{"id":"w13thu_q50","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Rosacea low-dose doxycycline","stem":"A 34-year-old has papules and pustules limited mostly to the cheeks and nose, where the background skin is erythematous and sensitive. The dermatologist prescribes low-dose doxycycline even though the patient's presentation does not resemble a typical bacterial follicular infection. Which property of the prescribed regimen explains its benefit in this disease?","options":["Direct suppression of follicular bacterial growth as its primary effect","Anti-inflammatory action at subantimicrobial doses","Alpha-2 receptor stimulation with vasoconstriction","Normalization of follicular plugging","Direct elimination of Demodex mites"],"answer":"B","expl":["At subantimicrobial doses the rosacea benefit is mainly anti-inflammatory","Low-dose doxycycline is used in rosacea mainly for anti-inflammatory rather than antibacterial effects","This is brimonidine action","This is topical retinoid acne action","This is topical ivermectin action"]}];